WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.540
A growth factor binds a receptor at the cell surface.

2
00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:12.300
Ras switches on, then passes the signal through three kinases: Raf, MEK and ERK.

3
00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:17.780
ERK acts on proteins in the cytoplasm and transcription factors in the nucleus.

4
00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:21.040
That changes gene expression and cell behaviour.

5
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:26.200
This is how a signal outside reaches the cell’s proteins and genes.

6
00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:29.500
Mutations that hold the relay on help drive cancer.

7
00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:32.520
So first learn what each step does to the next.

8
00:00:33.533 --> 00:00:36.993
The active receptor recruits Grb2 and SOS.

9
00:00:38.173 --> 00:00:41.533
SOS makes Ras release GDP so GTP can bind.

10
00:00:42.693 --> 00:00:43.693
Ras is not a kinase.

11
00:00:44.473 --> 00:00:46.533
It catches Raf and brings it to the membrane.

12
00:00:47.593 --> 00:00:49.273
Binding alone does not open Raf.

13
00:00:50.053 --> 00:00:53.433
Contact with membrane lipids and further steps help it open.

14
00:00:54.313 --> 00:00:58.093
Two Raf molecules then pair, which is needed for normal activation.

15
00:00:59.267 --> 00:01:01.807
Raf adds phosphates to two serines on MEK.

16
00:01:02.547 --> 00:01:05.967
MEK adds one to a threonine and one to a tyrosine on ERK.

17
00:01:06.807 --> 00:01:11.047
ERK needs both. The narrow relay ends in a wide fan of targets.

18
00:01:12.007 --> 00:01:16.847
In the nucleus, ERK phosphorylates Elk-1, which helps switch on the immediate early

19
00:01:16.847 --> 00:01:17.847
gene c-fos.

20
00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:22.060
In cancer, mutations can hold Ras or Raf on.

21
00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:26.320
B-Raf V600E can signal without Ras-GTP.

22
00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:29.000
A Raf inhibitor can block it.

23
00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:34.500
But with normal B-Raf and active Ras, drug-bound Raf can activate a drug-free

24
00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:37.060
partner. ERK activity then rises.

25
00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:40.640
A MEK inhibitor holds MEK inactive below Raf.

26
00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:43.800
The drug’s effect depends on the pathway’s state.

27
00:01:44.783 --> 00:01:46.683
The relay also limits itself.

28
00:01:47.643 --> 00:01:50.603
ERK weakens SOS and Raf by phosphorylation.

29
00:01:51.503 --> 00:01:56.103
It turns on genes for phosphatases that remove ERK’s activating phosphates.

30
00:01:56.843 --> 00:01:59.643
That takes longer, because a protein must be made.

31
00:02:00.723 --> 00:02:04.123
Signal duration also depends on the stimulus, cell type and dose.

32
00:02:05.143 --> 00:02:06.303
Timing helps shape the response.

33
00:02:07.317 --> 00:02:11.377
Three kinase tiers give amplification and separate control points.

34
00:02:12.217 --> 00:02:16.057
Other MAP kinase pathways use the same plan with different kinases.

35
00:02:17.237 --> 00:02:21.337
Scaffolds hold a module’s kinases together, helping them signal mainly to each

36
00:02:21.337 --> 00:02:24.697
other. They also help decide where the cascade runs.

37
00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:28.310
Milder over-activation also matters.

38
00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:34.010
Mutations inherited or new at conception cause developmental syndromes called

39
00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:37.350
RASopathies. Their effects differ between tissues.

40
00:02:38.270 --> 00:02:42.470
The relay carries the signal, but its control and the cell’s context shape the

41
00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:42.790
answer.
